<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso XIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso XIII of Spain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso XIII was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from his birth in 1886 until 1931. His reign followed the loss of the last colonies in 1898 and saw the costly war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the disaster at Annual in 1921, and growing labour unrest. He accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in 1923, which discredited the monarchy. After republicans won the municipal elections of April 1931 he left Spain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii.jpg" alt="Alfonso XIII" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaulak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rey_Alfonso_XIII_de_Espa%C3%B1a,_by_Kaulak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfonso XIII was born at the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 17 May 1886, the posthumous son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>, who had died in November 1885, and so he became king at birth. His mother, Maria Christina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, served as regent until his sixteenth birthday in 1902. During the regency, in 1898, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>.</p><p>He received largely a military education, which gave him a Spanish nationalism strengthened by a military vocation, and he often presented himself as a soldier-king. He took his oath to the constitution before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> on 17 May 1902. In 1906 he married Princess Victoria Eugenie of Battenberg, and their wedding was marred by an attempt on the king's life, from which he emerged unharmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Like other European monarchs of his time Alfonso played a political role, which involved a controversial use of his constitutional powers. He helped to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, using his links with other royal families, and was nominated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1917 for his work with the European War Office.</p><p>Several factors weakened his constitutional legitimacy: the breakdown of the turno system, the crisis of the Restoration system in the 1910s, a trio of crises in 1917, and the violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a> was installed in 1923 through a military coup, and the king acquiesced.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> failed, Alfonso withdrew his support, Primo de Rivera resigned in 1930, and the king tried to return to the position before 1923, but he had lost most of his political capital. On 12 April 1931 a republican coalition won a sweeping majority in the major cities in the municipal elections, which were seen as a plebiscite on the monarchy. The Civil Guard's José Sanjurjo reportedly told ministers that the armed forces could not be relied on to sustain the monarchy. Alfonso left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> was peacefully proclaimed on 14 April 1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In November 1931 the Constituent Cortes debated his responsibility as king, citing interference in state institutions, his use of the army, and his part in the operation that ended in the disaster at Annual. It passed an act declaring him guilty of high treason.</p><p>On 15 January 1941 Alfonso renounced his rights to the Spanish throne in favour of his son Juan, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 28 February 1941 of angina pectoris. His remains were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a> in January 1980. He promoted tourism, including state-run lodges called Paradores, and patronised football clubs that took the title Real, the first being Deportivo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">La Coruña</a> in 1907.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso XIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two days later the Second Republic was proclaimed, King <b>Alfonso XIII</b> went into exile, and a provisional government was formed under Niceto Alcalá-Zamora.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Alfonso XIII</b> a Royal Circular Order of 27 August 1908 made the orchestration by Bartolomé Pérez Casas, musician of the Royal Corps of Halberdier Guards, the official version.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li><li><blockquote>After republicans swept the cities in municipal elections, the Republic was declared and <b>Alfonso XIII</b> left Spain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alfonso XIII</b> backed Miguel Primo de Rivera's dictatorship from 1923 to 1930.</blockquote><a href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Royal Palace of Madrid, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.71&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Royal Palace of Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfonso XIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.71&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
